WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.680
Most metabolic pathways fall into one of two camps, and keeping them straight will

2
00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.000
carry you through the whole course.

3
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:12.140
Catabolic pathways break large molecules down.

4
00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:16.920
They combust carbon fuels like carbohydrates and fats to carbon dioxide and water,

5
00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:21.260
and they capture the released energy as ATP or as ion gradients.

6
00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:24.040
Anabolic pathways run the other direction.

7
00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:29.280
They take simple precursors and spend energy and reducing power to build complex

8
00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.940
biomolecules. So catabolism releases energy by tearing down.

9
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.920
Anabolism consumes energy to assemble.

10
00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:42.080
The cell is constantly running both at once, with the products of one feeding the

11
00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:43.080
other.

12
00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:47.160
A reaction that won't go on its own can be forced uphill if we link it to one that

13
00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.160
wants to go downhill.

14
00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:53.900
On the left, A to B plus C has a positive delta G of plus 21, so alone it stalls.

15
00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:59.000
On the right, we couple it to a strongly favorable reaction with a delta G of minus

16
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:05.360
34. Add them, and the net delta G is minus 13, comfortably negative, so the combined

17
00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:08.940
pathway proceeds. This is the central trick of metabolism.

18
00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:14.920
Individual steps must be specific, and each step's real delta G has to be negative

19
00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:19.540
for forward flux. Coupling is how the cell pays for the reactions it needs.

20
00:01:20.583 --> 00:01:25.263
ATP hydrolysis is exergonic and four distinct effects explain why.

21
00:01:25.903 --> 00:01:27.763
First, charge repulsion drops.

22
00:01:28.443 --> 00:01:32.843
The triphosphate tail carries several closely packed negative charges and cutting it

23
00:01:32.843 --> 00:01:34.203
apart relieves that strain.

24
00:01:35.063 --> 00:01:39.923
Second, resonance stabilization increases because inorganic phosphate has more

25
00:01:39.923 --> 00:01:43.303
equivalent resonance forms than the phosphate locked inside ATP.

26
00:01:43.303 --> 00:01:46.803
Third, entropy rises since one molecule becomes two.

27
00:01:47.583 --> 00:01:51.623
Fourth, the products are better stabilized by water through hydration than the

28
00:01:51.623 --> 00:01:55.303
reactant was. Together, they make hydrolysis favorable.

29
00:01:56.263 --> 00:02:01.723
Crucially, ATP is thermodynamically unstable but kinetically stable, so it doesn't

30
00:02:01.723 --> 00:02:03.903
waste itself until an enzyme calls on it.

31
00:02:04.833 --> 00:02:07.853
Here's the payoff of coupling, under standard conditions.

32
00:02:08.633 --> 00:02:12.573
An unfavorable reaction sitting alone has an equilibrium constant of about one

33
00:02:12.573 --> 00:02:15.013
one-thousandth, so almost no product forms.

34
00:02:15.733 --> 00:02:18.513
Now couple that same reaction to ATP hydrolysis.

35
00:02:19.113 --> 00:02:24.013
The favorable energy of hydrolysis adds in, the combined equilibrium constant jumps

36
00:02:24.013 --> 00:02:27.313
to roughly 267, and product now dominates.

37
00:02:28.093 --> 00:02:32.453
That's a shift of about 10 to the fifth, a hundred-thousand-fold change in where the

38
00:02:32.453 --> 00:02:38.113
reaction settles. This single idea is why ATP is the cell's universal coupling

39
00:02:38.113 --> 00:02:43.713
agent. Spending one phosphoanhydride bond can drag an otherwise hopeless reaction

40
00:02:43.713 --> 00:02:45.013
far toward products.

41
00:02:46.083 --> 00:02:50.543
The cell monitors its own energy state with a number called the energy charge, and

42
00:02:50.543 --> 00:02:53.083
it works like a fuel gauge running from zero to one.

43
00:02:53.743 --> 00:02:58.663
The formula weights ATP fully and ADP half, then divides by the total adenylate

44
00:02:58.663 --> 00:03:03.523
pool, so a cell loaded with ATP reads near the top and a depleted cell reads low.

45
00:03:04.263 --> 00:03:05.683
This value helps steer flux.

46
00:03:06.383 --> 00:03:11.083
When the charge is high, the cell throttles down catabolic ATP-generating pathways

47
00:03:11.083 --> 00:03:13.963
and turns up anabolic ATP-spending ones.

48
00:03:14.603 --> 00:03:16.983
When the charge falls, it does the reverse.

49
00:03:17.843 --> 00:03:22.243
Energy charge is a key signal balancing energy production against energy

50
00:03:22.243 --> 00:03:22.703
consumption.
